{"name":"Fauna","hex":"#c59daa","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Fauna","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["The goddess of animals, nature, spring and fertility; she is also the consort of Faunus.","A female given name from Latin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Fauna"}],"entries":[{"title":"Fauna","blocks":[{"text":"Fauna is all the animal life present in a particular region or time. The corresponding terms for plants and fungi are flora and funga, respectively. Flora, fauna, funga and other forms of life are collectively referred to as biota. Zoologists and paleontologists use fauna to refer to a typical collection of animals found in a specific time or place, e.g. the \"Sonoran Desert fauna\" or the \"Burgess Shale fauna\". Paleontologists sometimes refer to a sequence of faunal stages, which is a series of rocks all containing similar fossils. The study of animals of a particular region is called faunistics."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Fauna.png/330px-Fauna.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fauna"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Australisk_fauna%2C_Nordisk_familjebok.jpg/500px-Australisk_fauna%2C_Nordisk_familjebok.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Australisk_fauna%2C_Nordisk_familjebok.jpg/1280px-Australisk_fauna%2C_Nordisk_familjebok.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Australisk_fauna%2C_Nordisk_familjebok.jpg/500px-Australisk_fauna%2C_Nordisk_familjebok.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Papuan, Australian and New Zealand fauna. This image was likely first published in the first edition (1